DevOps Internship - Week 6 Report

Topic :   Infrastructure as Code & Configuration Management

-> Introduction
This week’s focus was on automating infrastructure creation and configuration
using Terraform and Ansible. Instead of manually setting up servers, I learned how to define
them as code — a crucial step toward scalable, repeatable DevOps environments.

-> Tools & Concepts
Purpose Infrastructure as Code Automate infrastructure setup through code Terraform
Provision cloud resources Ansible Configure and manage servers Variables & Outputs Make
configurations dynamic and reusable Terraform StateTrack resource changes Modules
Enable modular, maintainable IaC Playbooks & Inventory Define tasks and target hosts

-> Practical Implementation
Terraform Installed Terraform and configured AWS provider.Created a .tf file defining an EC2
instance.
Executed:
    terraform init
    terraform plan
    terraform apply
    terraform destroy
Verified infrastructure creation and destruction using state files.Ansible Installed Ansible and
created an inventory file.Developed a playbook to:Install Apache and required packages
Create a user deploy Start the web service Deploy sample web content

-> Workflow Diagram
Terraform → Infrastructure → Ansible → Server Configuration → ApplicationThis sequence
shows how Terraform provisions the environment and Ansible configures it automatically,
ensuring consistency and eliminating manual errors.

-> Key Learnings
Terraform handles provisioning, Ansible handles configuration.Always run terraform plan
before terraform apply.Modular IaC improves reusability and reduces maintenance.AI
assistants like Microsoft Copilot help generate, review, and optimize IaC code.

-> Conclusion
This week deepened my understanding of automation in DevOps. Combining Terraform and
Ansible demonstrated how declarative infrastructure and consistent configuration create
reliable cloud environments. These skills are vital for my growth as an DevOps Engineer